PART TWO:
Projects For All School Facilities. Unless otherwise noted, the following
projects are authorized to be completed as needed at each or any of the
District’s sites (including newly acquired or leased sites as authorized
hereunder):
Thriving School Communities
To support thriving school communities, the District may replace or upgrade
aging and inadequate classroom, student service, and science buildings.
The District may also replace any remaining outdated building structures.
Athletic facilities, playfields, and sports fields to support student health,
wellness, extracurricular programs, and physical education to be
constructed, renovated, or expanded. At elementary schools, provide natural
play areas with native and drought-tolerant plant species.
The District may also plan, acquire property for, design, and construct new
classrooms and school facilities to accommodate student enrollment in
neighborhood schools, including to accommodate growth in the following
high school cluster areas: Clairemont High School cluster, Crawford High
School cluster, Henry High School cluster, Hoover High School cluster,
Kearny High School cluster, La Jolla High School cluster, Lincoln High
School cluster, Logan Memorial Educational Campus cluster, Madison
High School cluster, Mira Mesa High School cluster, Mission Bay High
School cluster, Morse High School cluster, Point Loma High School
cluster, San Diego High School cluster, Scripps Ranch High School
cluster, Canyon Hills High School cluster, and University City High School
cluster.
Student Health, Safety and Security
To enhance student safety, the District may upgrade campus security
through improved lighting, door and gate hardware, fencing, cameras,
and modern emergency communication systems. Additional exterior
lighting will be provided to support after-school programs, and student
drop-off and pick-up zones will be built to improve pedestrian safety and
traffic flow. Shade structures will be installed at elementary school
playgrounds. Food service areas and kitchens will be upgraded or expanded
to provide students with better access to healthy and nutritious meals. Fire
alarm and sprinkler systems to be upgraded to meet current safety
standards. Asbestos, lead-based paint, mold, and other hazardous
materials will be safely removed and abated.
Early Learning
To support the needs of our youngest students, the District may
renovate or construct new transitional kindergarten and/or early
education classrooms, restrooms, and playgrounds.
Energy Efficiency and Sustainability
To advance energy efficiency and sustainability, the District may implement
solar power, battery energy storage systems, and other technologies to
reduce climate change impacts. Electric vehicle charging stations will be
installed to support the District’s clean transportation goals and reduce
greenhouse gas emissions. Aging HVAC (heating, ventilating, and air
conditioning) equipment will be replaced, and LED (light-emitting diode)
fixtures will be installed to further increase energy efficiency and reduce
the District’s environmental footprint.
Major Repair and Replacement
To address major repair and replacement needs, the District may renovate
or replace outdated school facilities, including repairing or replacing aged
plumbing, pipes, and fixtures; upgrading electrical systems, wiring, and
lighting; replacing old doors and windows; and replacing HVAC systems to
enhance indoor air quality and energy efficiency. Deteriorated interior and
exterior walls, floors, and ceilings, as well as outdoor sidewalks and paved
areas, to be repaired or replaced. Aged building materials, dry rot, and
termite damage will also be addressed. Leaky roof systems will be repaired
or replaced.
Cracked, leaky, or deteriorated plumbing, stormwater, sewer, and other site
utility systems to be repaired or replaced. Worn or cracked elementary
school playground surfaces to be addressed. Multipurpose rooms and
athletic facilities at elementary and middle schools to be renovated as
needed. Stadiums, gymnasiums, sports fields and tracks, and other athletic
facilities at high schools to be improved or replaced as needed.
Accessibility and Code Compliance
To provide accessibility and ensure compliance with ADA (Americans with
Disabilities Act), the District may improve access for persons with
disabilities and ensure adherence to federal and state regulations. Aging
portable classrooms will be replaced or repaired to maintain code
compliance, and restroom facilities will be renovated and upgraded to meet
the building code. Physical education facilities, playgrounds, and fields will
be modernized to improve student safety.
Innovations in Education and Technology
To foster innovation in education and technology, the District may provide
science, technology, engineering, arts, and math (STEAM) labs; maker
spaces; and collaborative learning environments. Facilities for special
education students to be provided, expanded, or improved. Current
classroom and instructional technology and equipment will be provided to
support student learning. Wired and wireless technology infrastructure to be
upgraded Districtwide, including charter schools.
Visual and Performing Arts
To support Visual and Performing Arts (VAPA) education, the District may
construct new or renovate existing VAPA facilities. The District may also
plan, acquire or lease property for, design, and construct new VAPA
classrooms and school facilities.
